 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Structure",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A buried Zener diode reference, a buffer amplifier and laser-trimmed thin-film resistors make a 5 V reference with an initial error of 2.0 mV or less and a drift under 2 ppm/°C in the best grade. A 4 kΩ resistor RS between the Zener and the amplifier's non-inverting input reaches the NOISE REDUCTION pin, so a capacitor on that pin makes a low-pass filter for the Zener."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Basic use",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Supply on pin 2, ground on pin 4, and pin 6 gives 5 V with no external parts; accuracy is chosen by grade. Quiescent current is under 3 mA from a 12 V or 15 V supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Fine trim",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A 10 kΩ potentiometer across VOUT and GND with its wiper on TRIM shifts the output by up to 300 mV with little effect on other characteristics, for calibration to exactly 5.000 V or to another value such as 5.12 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Noise",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Noise is typically under 4 µV p-p from 0.1 Hz to 10 Hz and about 200 µV p-p in a 1 MHz bandwidth, nearly all from the Zener at about 100 nV/√Hz; the amplifier adds little. The 0.1 Hz to 10 Hz figure uses a single-pole 0.1 Hz high-pass and a two-pole 12.6 Hz low-pass, a 9.922 Hz bandwidth. A 1 µF capacitor from NOISE REDUCTION to ground, with RS and the Zener resistance, places a 3 dB point at 12 Hz and cuts 1 MHz-bandwidth noise to about 160 µV p-p."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Turn-on",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "From a cold start the output settles to 0.01% in about 60 µs, with no thermal tail out to the millisecond scale. A noise reduction capacitor loads the Zener's current source and lengthens turn-on: with 1 µF, about 400 ms to 0.01%."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Dynamic load",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The output buffer is measured stepping between 0 mA and 10 mA, with and without 1000 pF of load capacitance, as the transient loads of ADCs and DACs and long capacitive cables would."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Load regulation",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "A change of a few mA in load shifts the output only a few µV; regulation is better when sourcing than when sinking."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature testing",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Drift is specified as an error box: the output is measured at three or more temperatures, and the diagonal of the box bounded by the temperature extremes and the highest and lowest output sets the grade. J, K and L units are tested at 0°C, 25°C and 70°C; SQ and TQ units at −55°C, +25°C and +125°C. Where the box sits vertically varies from unit to unit with initial error and curve shape. The AD586M deviates from 5.000 V by at most ±2.45 mV from 0°C to 70°C; the AD586T has a total error of at most ±7.5 mV from −55°C to +125°C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Negative reference",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "With VOUT grounded, VIN on at least 6 V and GND pulled to −15 V through RS, the GND pin gives −5.000 V. The current through the part must stay between 2.5 mA and 10.0 mA. Temperature behaviour and long-term stability match the positive connection."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Stacking",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Stacked parts give several references; with three, 5 V, 10 V and 15 V. A supply change from 18 V to 50 V moves the 5 V output by less than the reference noise."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grades and temperature ranges",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "J, K, L and M grades are specified from 0°C to 70°C, A and B from −40°C to +85°C, S and T from −55°C to +125°C. J, K, L and M come in 8-lead PDIP; J, K, L, A and B in 8-lead SOIC; J, K, L, S and T in 8-lead CERDIP. MIL-STD-883 versions exist."
  }
 ],
